    bigIdea: 'Here is the question that separates people who understand bonds from people who merely own them: if I am promised a fixed payment on a fixed date, why does the price of my bond change at all? The answer is one mechanism and it takes a minute to see. THE PROMISE CANNOT MOVE. It was written down. So when the return available elsewhere changes, the only thing left that CAN move is the price somebody will pay for that unchangeable promise. If new loans are paying more than yours, nobody wants yours at full price, so its price falls until the total return matches. If new loans are paying less, yours is worth more. PRICE AND PREVAILING RATES MOVE IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, always, and not because anybody decided it — because the promise is fixed. Scripture has the sharpest frame for a fixed promise in a moving world: "When thou vowest a vow unto God, defer not to pay it" (Ecclesiastes 5:4).',
