        kind: 'testimony', tone: 'serious',
        title: 'The Letter On The Page',
        body: 'A woman who worked in a pension office through 2008 said the thing she found hardest to explain afterwards was that nobody in her building had done anything reckless. They had a rule: the top grade or nothing. They followed it. Everything they had bought carried the top grade on the day they bought it, from firms whose entire business was assessing exactly that. And a great deal of it failed. She said the lesson she took was not that the agencies were villains, which she did not believe, and not that the letters were worthless, which she also did not believe. It was that she had stopped thinking somewhere along the line. The rule had been doing the thinking for her — the letter on the page was the whole of the analysis — and she could not now point to a single meeting where anybody had asked what would have to be true for the letter to be wrong. She said she has asked that question about every rule she has followed since, in work and out of it.',
