    lesson: 'The previous session assumed the borrower performs, which is the assumption everything else rests on. Take it away and you have this session, and it is the one that decides whether any of the arithmetic ever mattered. A BOND IS A PROMISE, AND A PROMISE IS WORTH PRECISELY WHAT ITS MAKER CAN AND WILL DELIVER. The trade names this CREDIT, and assessing it is far less arcane than the vocabulary suggests. THREE ORDINARY QUESTIONS DO MOST OF THE WORK. FIRST: what does the borrower earn, and how reliably? Steady income services a great deal of debt; lumpy income services much less of it safely, even when the annual totals match. SECOND: what does it already owe, to whom, and on what dates? Not only to you — the queue matters, and so does whether several large obligations fall due in the same year. THIRD: WHAT BECOMES OF BOTH IF THE NEXT SEVERAL YEARS GO BADLY? That is the only difficult question and it is the one routinely skipped, because on an ordinary day every obligation looks serviceable. Obligations are not tested on ordinary days. A FRACTION ANY READER CAN COMPUTE, and it is worth doing by hand once: annual interest divided by annual income. That is the proportion of everything the borrower earns which is already committed before anything else is paid for. Where it is small, a poor year is absorbed quietly. Where it is large, a poor year becomes a crisis — and it arrives faster than people expect, because the payments do not pause while the situation is reconsidered. ON THE RATING AGENCIES THIS COURSE WILL BE PLAIN, in both directions. Firms exist whose business is publishing an opinion on exactly this question, in letter grades, and that opinion is genuinely useful: it compresses a great deal of analysis into something comparable across thousands of issuers, and a reader should absolutely use it. IT IS NOT A GUARANTEE, and the reasons are documented rather than suspected. In the dominant commercial model, THE AGENCY IS PAID BY THE ISSUER WHOSE DEBT IT IS RATING — a structural conflict that every party involved acknowledges openly. And in the financial crisis of 2008, very large quantities of debt carried the highest available grades and subsequently failed. Stating that plainly is not cynicism about the institutions, and it is emphatically not a theory about anybody’s motives; it is the record. A course that softened it into "some have raised questions" would be failing the reader in the direction that costs money — and under-claiming a documented harm is as much a failure of truth as over-claiming an undocumented one. USE THE RATINGS AS ONE INPUT. DO NOT TRANSFER THE JUDGEMENT INTO THEM. The useful discipline is the one the second story names: ask what would have to be true for the letter to be wrong, and notice if nobody in the room has asked it. AND SCRIPTURE DESCRIBES THE POSITION WITHOUT ANY SENTIMENT WHATEVER. "The rich ruleth over the poor, and the borrower is servant to the lender." (Proverbs 22:7) NOTICE WHAT KIND OF SENTENCE THAT IS. It is not a command and it is not a prohibition. It does not forbid borrowing, it does not forbid lending, and reading it as though it did is exactly the over-reach this house refuses — a true thing loses its authority when it is stretched to cover ground it never claimed. It is a DESCRIPTION of where the power sits once money has moved, set down so that a person may know it in advance rather than discover it. Anyone who has been the borrower in a hard year will tell you the verse is exact. And anyone intending to be the lender should read it as a statement about what they are becoming to somebody — which is where lesson eight begins.',
