    stories: [
      {
        kind: 'testimony', tone: 'serious',
        title: 'She Signed Nothing',
        body: 'A nurse who worked through her country’s restructuring said the reporting was accurate and completely unrecognisable. Every figure she read was correct. The recovery rate was correct. The projections were, as far as she could tell, honest. And none of it contained the ward. Her hospital had stopped receiving one imported medicine in the March and a different one in the June, and the substitutions were not as good, and everybody knew they were not as good, and there was no one to be angry at because the supplier had not done anything wrong and neither had the pharmacist. She said what she could not get past was a woman in her sixties who had worked forty-one years and whose pension had been reduced twice, and who apologised to the staff for being a burden. She said that woman had never in her life signed a contract in a foreign currency, had never voted for the government that did, and was the person paying. She said the recovery rate for the bondholders was, she looked it up, rather good.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'parable', tone: 'serious',
        title: 'And Live Of The Rest',
        body: 'A man in a small group said he had always disliked the story of the widow’s oil, and when asked why he said because it seemed to let the creditor off. Somebody was taking her sons and the answer was a pot of oil. They read it again slowly. He noticed for the first time that Elisha never speaks to the creditor at all, and that the debt is paid rather than forgiven, and he said that made it worse. Then somebody read the last clause again — live thou and thy children of the rest — and he stopped. He said he had read that passage a dozen times and had never once registered that there was a rest. He had always ended the story at "pay thy debt", because that was the hard part and he was the sort of man who noticed hard parts. He said he had been carrying a version of this story for thirty years in which provision arrives, clears what you owe, and leaves you empty. He said that was his own doctrine and not the text’s, and he had not known the difference until that evening.',
      },
    ],
    lesson: 'When a sovereign borrower cannot meet its obligations, the outcome is usually less dramatic than expected and far more consequential than reported. THERE IS A NEGOTIATION. Creditors accept a reduced principal, or a longer term, or a lower rate, or some combination; an agreement is signed; the financial coverage records a recovery rate and moves on. That process is real, it is how most of these episodes actually conclude, and understanding it is worth something — a reader should know that the usual end of a sovereign debt crisis is a document rather than a collapse. IT IS ALSO THE LEAST IMPORTANT HALF, AND THIS SESSION SAYS SO PLAINLY. Scripture does not describe a debt crisis in aggregate. It shows one, and it shows it from underneath. "Now there cried a certain woman of the wives of the sons of the prophets unto Elisha, saying, Thy servant my husband is dead; and thou knowest that thy servant did fear the LORD: and the creditor is come to take unto him my two sons to be bondmen." (2 Kings 4:1) TAKE THE ELEMENTS ONE AT A TIME, because every one of them is load-bearing. IT OPENS WITH A CRY — that is how this subject sounds from underneath, and it is never how it sounds in a report. THE HUSBAND IS DEAD, AND THE TEXT IS CAREFUL TO SAY HE FEARED THE LORD. This is not a cautionary tale about a spendthrift, and a teacher who turns it into one has inverted the passage to make it more comfortable. THE DEBT OUTLIVED HIM and fell on somebody who did not contract it. THE CREDITOR IS, so far as the text states, ACTING WITHIN HIS ENTITLEMENT; there is no allegation of fraud anywhere in the verse. AND TWO CHILDREN ARE ABOUT TO BE TAKEN in satisfaction. WHAT THAT CORRESPONDS TO in a modern sovereign default is not hard to trace and is rarely traced: pensions purchasing less each month, hospitals unable to buy imported medicine, schools without materials, employment collapsing in whatever sectors depended on the state, and a generation of people who signed nothing carrying the cost of arrangements made by people who did. STATING THAT IS NOT SENSATIONALISM AND THIS COURSE WILL NOT HEDGE IT. Equally it will not inflate it into the claim that every creditor is a predator or that no obligation should be honoured, because neither is true and neither is in this text — and a reader given only the grievance has been disarmed as thoroughly as a reader given only the recovery rate. AND ELISHA’S RESPONSE IS WORTH AS MUCH AS THE DIAGNOSIS, because it is not what most readers expect. HE DOES NOT DENOUNCE THE CREDITOR — he never addresses him at all. HE DOES NOT DECLARE THE OBLIGATION VOID. He asks what is in the house; there is a pot of oil; and after the provision comes, his instruction has two halves in one sentence. "Go, sell the oil, and pay thy debt, and live thou and thy children of the rest." (2 Kings 4:7) PAY THY DEBT. The miracle does not dissolve the obligation — it makes the obligation payable, which is a different mercy and a more serious one. AND LIVE THOU AND THY CHILDREN OF THE REST. The provision EXCEEDED the debt, and the excess was hers to live on. A reader who carries only the first half has a hard doctrine. A reader who carries only the second has a sentimental one. The passage gives both, in that order, in a single sentence, and anyone teaching it should read it to the end.',
