        body: 'A man who taught this to a night class said the hardest part was stopping people at exactly the right moment. He would explain the mechanism and you could watch the room arrive at it together — the money is worth less, the debt is easier, the borrower gained, the lender lost — and then, every single time, somebody would say: so they are doing it on purpose. And he would say, that is the wrong next sentence and it is the sentence that ends your thinking. He would ask them to name who "they" were and what meeting had been held. Usually nobody could. He said the point was not that intent never exists anywhere; it was that the arithmetic works identically whether anybody intends it or not, which is a far more useful and far more uncomfortable thing to know. He said a room that leaves angry has a story. A room that leaves with a question — what is the real return — has something they can use on a Tuesday.',
